From c5febf64f6daa2fbcd70ee4416a6a45391f786af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Somalapuram Amaranath <somalapuram@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 10:53:04 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] audio: don't discard a configured built-in mic address

createAudioDevice() reads the devicePort address out of the XML and then
overwrites it with "bottom" for every IN_MICROPHONE whose connection is
empty (and "back" for IN_MICROPHONE_BACK). The two literals look like
harmless positional hints, but the address is load-bearing:
StreamPrimary::getCardAndDeviceId() rfind()s "CARD_" in it and sscanf()s
"CARD_%d_DEV_%d" from there, falling back to kDefaultCardAndDeviceId --
PrimaryMixer::kAlsaCard 0, kAlsaDevice 0 -- when there is no match.

So the clobber pins every built-in mic to ALSA card 0 device 0 and
silently discards the only mechanism a config author has for saying
otherwise. On a phone that is harmless. On a PC it is not: card 0
device 0 is whatever the HDA codec enumerates first, typically the
analog mic jack, while the internal mics are a separate DMIC PCM. With
nothing plugged into the jack, every recording is its noise floor, and
nothing anywhere reports an error.

Measured on an HP ZBook Firefly 16 G11 (sof-hda-dsp), playing a 1 kHz
tone through the speaker and capturing with tinycap:

    card 0 device 0  "HDA Analog"  ambient RMS -66.5 dBFS
                                   with tone  -66.6 dBFS   (+0.0 dB)
    card 0 device 6  "DMIC Raw"    ambient RMS -60.2 dBFS
                                   with tone  -29.1 dBFS  (+31.1 dB)

Only default the address when the config did not supply one. A config
that says nothing keeps the previous value, so no existing device
changes behaviour.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01TEuEUJYn8gjRcyxBBzgcEN
Change-Id: I2025f00b295d3c59356b86e5c0b091b1fe2743f3
---
 audio/aidl/default/XsdcConversion.cpp | 22 +++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/audio/aidl/default/XsdcConversion.cpp b/audio/aidl/default/XsdcConversion.cpp
index fbda7aac5e..bfe30d9c10 100644
--- a/audio/aidl/default/XsdcConversion.cpp
+++ b/audio/aidl/default/XsdcConversion.cpp
@@ -236,11 +236,23 @@ ConversionResult<AudioDevice> createAudioDevice(
                                ? AudioDeviceAddress::make<AudioDeviceAddress::Tag::id>(
                                          xDevicePort.getAddress())
                                : AudioDeviceAddress{}};
-    if (device.type.type == AudioDeviceType::IN_MICROPHONE && device.type.connection.empty()) {
-        device.address = "bottom";
-    } else if (device.type.type == AudioDeviceType::IN_MICROPHONE_BACK &&
-               device.type.connection.empty()) {
-        device.address = "back";
+    // Only default the address when the config did not give one. These two
+    // literals are positional hints for the common phone layout, but the address
+    // is not decorative: StreamPrimary::getCardAndDeviceId() rfind()s "CARD_" in
+    // it and sscanf()s "CARD_%d_DEV_%d" out, falling back to card 0 device 0
+    // otherwise. Overwriting an address the config author wrote therefore pins
+    // every built-in mic to ALSA card 0 device 0 and silently discards the only
+    // mechanism there is for saying otherwise -- which on a PC, where device 0
+    // is typically the analog jack and the internal mics are a separate DMIC
+    // PCM, means recording the noise floor of an empty socket.
+    if (!xDevicePort.hasAddress()) {
+        if (device.type.type == AudioDeviceType::IN_MICROPHONE &&
+            device.type.connection.empty()) {
+            device.address = "bottom";
+        } else if (device.type.type == AudioDeviceType::IN_MICROPHONE_BACK &&
+                   device.type.connection.empty()) {
+            device.address = "back";
+        }
     }
     return device;
 }
